</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning all-bias-terms stands out among various <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT)</span> techniques, owing to its out-of-the-box usability and competitive performance, especially in low-data regimes. Bias-only fine-tuning has the potential for unprecedented parameter efficiency. However, the link between fine-tuning different bias terms (i.e., bias terms in the query, key, or value projections) and downstream performance remains unclear. The existing approaches, e.g., based on the magnitude of bias change or empirical Fisher information, provide limited guidance for selecting the particular bias term for effective fine-tuning. In this paper, we propose an approach for selecting the bias term to be fine-tuned, forming the foundation of our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">bias-efficient fine-tuning (BEFT)</span>. We extensively evaluate our bias-efficient approach against other bias-selection approaches, across a wide range of <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">large language models (LLMs)</span> spanning encoder-only and decoder-only architectures from 110M to 6.7B parameters. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness and superiority of our bias-efficient approach on diverse downstream tasks, including classification, multiple-choice, and generation tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Department of Electrical and Information Technology, Lund University, Sweden</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Google DeepMind, USA</p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">{baichuan.huang, amir.aminifar}@eit.lth.se, ananthbshankar@google.com</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning pre-trained <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">large language models (LLMs)</span> for various downstream tasks has gained a lot of attention over the past few years. <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT)</span> methods have been extensively and empirically investigated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> to reduce the fine-tuning overheads, such as computation cost, <span title="graphics processing unit" class="ltx_glossaryref">graphics processing unit (GPU)</span> memory usage, and energy consumption. Among these <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques, bias-only fine-tuning—which involves updating only the bias terms of the <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite>—provides the potential for unprecedented parameter efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Bias-only fine-tuning offers out-of-the-box usability, without extra requirements for additional configuration and auxiliary reparameterization. This is in contrast to the majority of the previous work in this domain, e.g., prefix tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang 2021</a>)</cite>, adapter tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Houlsby et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, and GaLore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Moreover, in low-data regimes, all-bias-terms fine-tuning has been shown to be competitive with full-parameter fine-tuning, despite updating a significantly smaller subset of full parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Logan IV et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doering et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15974v1/observation_amir_cpu.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/419;" width="698" height="419"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Importance ranking and accuracy (%) of <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">fine-tuning different bias terms (query <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, key <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and value <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>) using</span> various bias-selection approaches <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">on the SST-2 dataset with BERT<sub id="S1.F1.6.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S1.F1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> (low-data: <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> training samples)</span>. <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We expect higher-ranked bias terms to achieve higher accuracy</span>. Our approach precisely and dynamically selects the particular bias terms <span id="S1.F1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">to be effectively fine-tuned</span>, compared to Magnitude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite> and Fisher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung, Nair, and Raffel 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite> approaches.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the advantages of bias-only fine-tuning, arbitrarily selecting <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">different bias terms</span> for fine-tuning does not necessarily guarantee competitive performance. <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">At the same time</span>, the <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">link</span> between <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">fine-tuning different bias terms</span> and downstream performance remains unclear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, Kerrigan, and Yang 2024</a>)</cite>. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">we report the importance ranking of bias terms in query <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, key <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and value <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> among all bias terms, on the y-axis</span>. We observe that the magnitude of <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">bias change</span> before and after fine-tuning (Magnitude approach) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite> is inaccurate in selecting the particular bias term to be fine-tuned, especially in the low-data regime. At the same time, the widely used empirical Fisher information (Fisher approach) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung, Nair, and Raffel 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite> <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">leads to a static importance ranking, with the same importance ranking for bias terms from the low-data regime to the all-data regime</span>. Therefore, both approaches have limitations for selecting the particular bias term to be fine-tuned. These observations motivate us to investigate how to carefully select the <span id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">particular</span> bias term for <span id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">effective</span> fine-tuning across various data regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose an approach to select the particular bias term to be fine-tuned for <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">bias-efficient fine-tuning (BEFT)</span>. Inspired by recent advances in decoupling weight updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bini, Girrbach, and Akata 2025</a>)</cite>, our bias-efficient approach jointly considers both the angular change and magnitude of bias change before and after fine-tuning. Concretely, our proposed approach evaluates the projection ratios of bias before and after fine-tuning, providing a more precise and dynamic measure of bias change, compared to the existing Magnitude and Fisher approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We extensively evaluate our bias-efficient approach against the Magnitude and Fisher approaches on BERT<sub id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> using the GLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019b</a>)</cite>. Building on this, we extend our analysis to RoBERTa<sub id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S1.p5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> and BERT<sub id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S1.p5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LARGE</span></sub> on the SuperGLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019a</a>)</cite>. Finally, we generalize our findings to decoder-only (autoregressive) architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, with sizes up to 6.7B parameters. Our main contributions are summarized below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an approach to select the particular bias term for effective fine-tuning of downstream tasks. Using projection, our approach jointly considers the angular and magnitude changes, providing a more precise and dynamic measure compared to the existing Magnitude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite> and Fisher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung, Nair, and Raffel 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite> approaches.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extensively evaluate our bias-efficient approach across a wide range of <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> spanning masked and autoregressive architectures from 110M to 6.7B parameters. The results consistently demonstrate the effectiveness of our bias-efficient approach against the Magnitude and Fisher approaches on a diverse set of downstream tasks, including classification, multiple-choice, and generation tasks. Moreover, our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> achieves competitive performance compared to mainstream <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Bias-Selection Approaches</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLM</span> model with pre-trained parameters <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}^{|\boldsymbol{\theta}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}\in\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}^{|\boldsymbol{\theta}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, the output <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is inferred from the input <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the parameters <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. The full-parameter fine-tuning is to update all the parameters <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> for downstream tasks, including all weights <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{W}\in\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}^{|\boldsymbol{W}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{W}\in\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}^{|\boldsymbol{W}|}</annotation></semantics></math> and all biases <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}\in\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}^{|\boldsymbol{b}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}\in\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}^{|\boldsymbol{b}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the number of biases <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{|\boldsymbol{b}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{|\boldsymbol{b}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is substantially smaller than the number of weights <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{|\boldsymbol{W}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{|\boldsymbol{W}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, bias-only fine-tuning exclusively updates the bias terms of the <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, significantly reducing the number of trainable parameters during model adaptation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15974v1/method_amir.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/496;" width="698" height="496"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Our bias-efficient approach jointly considers both the angular change and magnitude change, to measure the projection ratio of <math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> (visualization in <math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15974v1/main_amir.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:1326/941;" width="1326" height="941"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Approaches to select <span id="S2.F3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">different bias terms</span> for fine-tuning: (a) the magnitude of bias change before and after fine-tuning. Different fine-tuned biases situated on the green rhombus exhibit the same Magnitude value; (b) the empirical Fisher information before fine-tuning. Different gradients
situated on the yellow circle result in the same Fisher value; (c) our bias-efficient approach, which circumvents the limitations encountered by Magnitude or Fisher approaches.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon bias-only fine-tuning, the bias-selection approaches aim to select the particular bias term, rather than all bias terms, for fine-tuning. To define the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">different types of</span> bias term, the collection of <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the single type of</span> bias <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> across Transformer layers <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is exploited as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}=\left\{\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)}\right\}^{L}_{l=1}\subseteq\boldsymbol{b}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}=\left\{\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)}\right\}^{L}_{l=1}\subseteq\boldsymbol{b},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}\in\mathcal{\boldsymbol{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}\in\mathcal{\boldsymbol{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\boldsymbol{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝓢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\boldsymbol{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the set of all types of bias terms</span>. In this paper, <span id="S2.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">we select one type of bias term among query <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, key <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and value <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we refer to as the <em id="S2.p2.2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target bias</em> term to be fine-tuned</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Our Bias-Efficient Approach</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As previously discussed and shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the existing bias-selection approaches remain limited in their guidance to select the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">particular</span> bias term for fine-tuning <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>. To investigate these limitations, we propose an approach that jointly takes into consideration both the angular change and magnitude change of bias before and after fine-tuning. Our bias-efficient approach considers the projection ratio as the importance score and selects the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">particular</span> bias term with the highest importance score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the bias term at layer <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math></span> before fine-tuning as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),pre}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),pre}</annotation></semantics></math> and after fine-tuning as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),post}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),post}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The bias-efficient approach jointly considers both the angular change and magnitude change, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. When the magnitude of fine-tuned bias is smaller than the magnitude of the bias before fine-tuning, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (a), the projection ratio of fine-tuned bias onto the bias before fine-tuning is considered to calculate the importance score:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)})=1-\frac{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}\cdot\cos(\alpha)}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}}=1-\frac{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\cdot\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)})=1-\frac{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}\cdot\cos(\alpha)}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}}=1-\frac{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\cdot\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the angle between the bias before and after fine-tuning. On the contrary, when the magnitude of fine-tuned bias is not smaller than the magnitude of the bias before fine-tuning, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (b), the projection ratio of the bias before fine-tuning onto the fine-tuned bias is considered to calculate the importance score:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)})=1-\frac{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}\cdot\cos(\alpha)}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}}=1-\frac{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\cdot\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l)})=1-\frac{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}\cdot\cos(\alpha)}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|_{2}}=1-\frac{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\cdot\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}}{\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Overall, the importance score <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> of <span id="S2.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">this bias term across all layers</span> is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})=\frac{1}{L}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\left(1-\frac{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\cdot\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}}{\max\left(\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2},\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2}\right)}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})=\frac{1}{L}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\left(1-\frac{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\cdot\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}}{\max\left(\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2},\|\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),post}_{\mathcal{T}}\|^{2}_{2}\right)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">To select the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias term, we compute importance scores <span id="S2.SS1.p2.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for all types of bias terms</span> and select the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.4.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target bias</span> type <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p2.4.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(among query, key, and value)</span> with the highest <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}=\underset{\mathcal{T}\in\{q,\ k,\ v\}}{\arg\max}{\{\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})\}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒯</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.577em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.577em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}=\underset{\mathcal{T}\in\{q,\ k,\ v\}}{\arg\max}{\{\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Comparison with Existing Approaches</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing studies on bias selection have primarily relied on a few approaches, such as the magnitude of bias change before and after fine-tuning (Magnitude) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite>, and the empirical Fisher information (Fisher) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung, Nair, and Raffel 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the magnitude of bias change before and after fine-tuning, the Manhattan distance, i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}-</annotation></semantics></math>norm, is exploited to quantify the change (i.e., importance score) of bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite>. Therefore, the importance score <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> of magnitude-based approaches is denoted as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})=\frac{1}{L}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\left\|\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),post}-\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),pre}\right\|_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})=\frac{1}{L}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\left\|\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),post}-\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{(l),pre}\right\|_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In this bias-selection approach (which does not consider angular changes), different fine-tuned biases situated on the rhombus exhibit the same magnitude of bias change (i.e., same importance score) as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (a), indicating that the Magnitude approach fails to capture meaningful variations. The same limitation is observed on the circle when the Euclidean distance (<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}-</annotation></semantics></math>norm) is exploited.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S2.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15974v1/SST2_bold_cpu.svg" id="S2.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/872;" width="698" height="872"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>SST-2</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S2.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15974v1/cola_bold.svg" id="S2.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/872;" width="698" height="872"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>CoLA</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S2.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15974v1/STSB_bold.svg" id="S2.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/872;" width="698" height="872"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>STS-B</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Importance ranking and downstream performance of fine-tuning different bias terms using various bias-selection approaches on (a) SST-2, (b) CoLA, and (c) STS-B, with BERT<sub id="S2.F4.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S2.F4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>. Overall, our bias-efficient approach demonstrates the ability to precisely and dynamically select <span id="S2.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">particular</span> bias across diverse data regimes, outperforming Magnitude and Fisher.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the Fisher information, <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the effect of changes in the bias before fine-tuning (<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{pre}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}}^{pre}</annotation></semantics></math>) on the output <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is measured, given training data <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Fisher information is typically approximated using its diagonal for computational efficiency, giving rise to the empirical Fisher information applied in supervised learning. Therefore, the importance score of Fisher is denoted as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})=\frac{1}{L\cdot N}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\nabla_{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}}\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{y}_{i}|\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\right)^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathcal{T}})=\frac{1}{L\cdot N}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\nabla_{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}}\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{y}_{i}|\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of training data. <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{y}_{i}|\boldsymbol{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{y}_{i}|\boldsymbol{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability distribution of the output <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLM</span> model parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and input <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}}\log p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{y}_{i}|\boldsymbol{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}_{\mathcal{T}}}\log p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{y}_{i}|\boldsymbol{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the gradient of the log-likelihood with respect to the bias <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}^{(l),pre}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, we observe that the importance ranking in Fisher stays mostly static during training. Therefore, we exploit the Fisher before actual fine-tuning, as done in previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung, Nair, and Raffel 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. In this bias-selection approach, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b), different gradients situated on the circle result in the identical gradient square (identical importance score). This approach struggles to effectively distinguish significant variations in empirical Fisher information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our bias-efficient approach provides a more precise and dynamic measure of bias change before and after fine-tuning compared to existing approaches. It not only circumvents the limitations encountered by the magnitude-based approaches, which assigns the same importance score to different fine-tuned biases with the same magnitude change as presented in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (a), but also avoids the drawbacks of approaches based on Fisher information, which yields the same importance score to different gradients as presented in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our bias-efficient approach for fine-tuning <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, we primarily exploit BERT<sub id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. 2019</a>)</cite> on the GLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019b</a>)</cite> without the WNLI task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite>. Building on this, we extend our analysis to RoBERTa<sub id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2019</a>)</cite> and BERT<sub id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LARGE</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. 2019</a>)</cite> on the GLUE benchmark and SuperGLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019a</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, to generalize our findings from masked <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> to autoregressive <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, we exploit OPT-1.3B and OPT-6.7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2022</a>)</cite> evaluated on the GLUE benchmark, SuperGLUE benchmark, SQuAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajpurkar et al. 2016</a>)</cite>, and DROP datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dua et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, across classification, multiple-choice, and generation tasks. All these experiments undergo training on 1 NVIDIA Tesla T4 <span title="graphics processing unit" class="ltx_glossaryref">GPU</span> with 16GB <span title="random-access memory" class="ltx_glossaryref">random-access memory (RAM)</span> for BERT<sub id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>, RoBERTa<sub id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>, and BERT<sub id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LARGE</span></sub>, with 1 NVIDIA Tesla A40 <span title="graphics processing unit" class="ltx_glossaryref">GPU</span> with 48GB <span title="random-access memory" class="ltx_glossaryref">RAM</span> for OPT-1.3B and 1 NVIDIA Tesla A100 <span title="graphics processing unit" class="ltx_glossaryref">GPU</span> with 80GB <span title="random-access memory" class="ltx_glossaryref">RAM</span> for OPT-6.7B.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
