</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Bias Selection Approaches</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the importance ranking of different bias terms and downstream performance for various bias-selection approaches, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Comparison with Existing Approaches ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The importance ranking is derived from importance scores of all bias terms.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Interpretative findings</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Comparison with Existing Approaches ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we present results on three representative datasets from the GLUE benchmark with distinct evaluation indicators, and we reveal the interpretative findings across different data regimes: low-, medium-, high-, and all-data regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the SST-2 dataset, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Comparison with Existing Approaches ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (a), our bias-efficient approach consistently selects <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias, while the Magnitude always selects <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Correspondingly, the accuracy associated with fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> surpasses that of <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> across different data regimes, showing the inaccuracy of the Magnitude—particularly in the low-data regime. In the case of the Fisher approach, although it also identifies <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias, it <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">results in a static importance ranking for bias terms across different data regimes</span>. In contrast, our bias-efficient approach dynamically captures the narrowing importance gap between <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as training data increases, aligning closely with the observed convergence in their downstream performance. These findings demonstrate that our bias-efficient approach not only achieves superior identification of <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias than Magnitude, but also has the capacity of dynamic importance ranking compared to Fisher approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for the CoLA dataset, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Comparison with Existing Approaches ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (b), <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistently selected as the <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias by our bias-efficient approach, while <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is always selected by the Magnitude approach. According to the performance metric (i.e., the Matthews correlation coefficient), the Magnitude approach selects the incorrect <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias between <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the Magnitude approach exhibits a static importance ranking across different data regimes. Similarly, the Fisher approach results in a <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">fixed importance ranking</span>. In contrast, our bias-efficient approach adaptively adjusts the performance gap between <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, aligning with the observed shift in their importance rankings. These consistent patterns highlight the superiority of our approach over both Magnitude and Fisher approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, for the STS-B dataset, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Comparison with Existing Approaches ‣ 2 Bias-Selection Approaches ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (c), the Magnitude approach selects <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias from medium- to all-data regimes. However, selecting <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> has a lower performance (i.e., lower Spearman correlation coefficient) than selecting <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although the Magnitude approach selects <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in the low-data regime, the importance ranking gap between <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> does not align with the observed differences in downstream performance, indicating a lack of adaptability. The Fisher approach, on the other hand, suffers from <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the static importance ranking</span> as before. Conversely, our bias-efficient approach reflects the increase performance gap between <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> from low- to medium-data regimes, in line with their changing importance ranking. These findings confirm the advantages of our bias-efficient approach over the Magnitude and Fisher approaches.</p>
</div>
