<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Empirical evaluation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we report the downstream performance for <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">different</span> bias terms (specifically, <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>). We annotate the <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias term selected by our bias-efficient approach with the symbol <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. As presented in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently achieves higher performance than fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> across the GLUE benchmark, from low- to high-data regimes. Conversely, fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> always underperforms compared to <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the importance ranking, our bias-efficient approach (<math id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>) consistently succeeds across all datasets and diverse training data regimes. Overall, these results show that our bias-efficient approach effectively captures importance rankings and selects the <span id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias term to be effectively fine-tuned, forming the foundation of our proposed <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Efficiency and Effectiveness</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prior work has indicated that fine-tuning all-bias-terms has competitive performance to full-parameter fine-tuning in low-data regimes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Logan IV et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doering et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. Building on these observations, we investigate the efficiency and effectiveness of our proposed <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> method in the low-data regime.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Interpretative findings ‣ 3.2 Bias Selection Approaches ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> achieves remarkable parameter efficiency, with only 0.01% of the full parameters when fine-tuning BERT<sub id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> on the low-data regime of the RTE dataset. Fine-tuning all biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite> requires 0.09% of the full parameters, nearly 9<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more trainable parameters. <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Although “rand uniform” samples and fine-tunes the same number of bias terms as <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span>, it yields substantially worse performance and requires more time due to inefficient uniform sampling.</span> In terms of runtime, full-parameter fine-tuning takes 206.1 <span title="seconds" class="ltx_glossaryref">seconds (s)</span>, and all-bias fine-tuning requires 144.9 <span title="seconds" class="ltx_glossaryref">s</span>, whereas our bias-efficient approach completes in just 132.9 <span title="seconds" class="ltx_glossaryref">s</span>. Despite this significant reduction in parameter footprint and a decrease in training time, our proposed <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> achieves a comparable performance with all-bias fine-tuning and all-parameter fine-tuning in this case.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Adaptation Techniques</th>
<th id="S3.T3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">SST-2</th>
<th id="S3.T3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">RTE</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">BERT<sub id="S3.T3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.T3.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> (without fine-tuning)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">48.9</td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">52.7</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Only-<math id="S3.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>-fine-tuned on RTE</td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">48.8</td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T3.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">59.5</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Only-<math id="S3.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>-fine-tuned on SST-2</td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T3.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">85.8</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">46.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Merge fine-tuned <math id="S3.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of RTE and SST-2</td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T3.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">81.3</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T3.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">55.5</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S3.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Merging task-specific <math id="S3.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒃</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> through arithmetic averaging improves adaptation performance (%), showing the effective adaptation of our selected target bias term.</span> </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we observe that the fine-tuned <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits task-specific characteristics for different tasks. For instance, the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in BERT<sub id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> after fine-tuning on SST-2 and RTE exhibit significant dissimilarity, with a cosine similarity of 0.18, i.e., an angular difference of 79.5<sup id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∘</span></sup>. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3 Efficiency and Effectiveness ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the model with only-<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>-fine-tuned on RTE exhibits worse performance when evaluated on SST-2, and vice versa. However, to investigate the adaptation effectiveness, merging task-specific <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> through arithmetic averaging improves adaptation performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our proposed <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> demonstrates a favorable trade-off between parameter percentage, runtime, and downstream performance, offering strong potential while fine-tuning only the <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias term <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">in <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span></span>. These findings highlight its promise as an unprecedented parameter-efficient fine-tuning strategy<span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, as well as the effective adaptation across tasks enabled by the task-arithmetic averaging of our selected target bias terms</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Extension Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the preceding interpretative findings and empirical evaluation, it is natural to ask: Can the insights gained from the <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias term be directly extended to different datasets and <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, without requiring additional measurement of bias change before and after fine-tuning?</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
