<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Downstream performance (%) of various fine-tuning techniques in low-data regimes. <span id="S3.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> approach demonstrates competitive performance with mainstream <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques in terms of parameter efficiency and downstream adaptation</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extend to different datasets</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the extension of our findings to new datasets in the low-data regime, we exploit the CB and WiC datasets from the SuperGLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019a</a>)</cite>, where the CB dataset contains only 250 training examples. First, we evaluate the same model, i.e., BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>, on these new datasets. As presented in Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 3.4 Extension Analysis ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, for BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>, fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is better than fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>; fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is better than fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. These results consistently mirror our preceding findings on the GLUE benchmark, showing that the observed patterns of the <span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">target</span> bias term can generalize to new datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond direct extension, we further investigate the internal correlation between our bias-efficient approach and the downstream performance. Specifically, we analyze the CB and WiC datasets under varying learning rates (e.g., <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>). We visualize the importance scores and importance rankings. Despite <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently being ranked first with different learning rates, the downstream performance varies significantly. For example, in CB dataset, while the ranking order of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> remains unchanged, the performance difference between <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is substantially larger at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> than at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the importance score gap between <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is wider at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> than at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We find that the importance score calculated by our bias-efficient approach provides a finer-grained approach than the importance ranking alone. Moreover, fine-tuning only <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> surpasses the performance of tuning all biases <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{all}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{all}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, but falls short at <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the higher importance score of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> at learning rate <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects greater change, leading to better performance when fine-tuning only <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. These results imply that learning rate influences the divergence degree of bias change, which in turn affects the effectiveness of <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span>; larger divergence (higher importance scores) tends to yield better performance. Similar trends are observed in the WiC dataset, reinforcing the robustness of our findings. Overall, this analysis shows that our bias-efficient offers provide fine-grained measurement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extend to different <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> </h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the extension of our findings to different <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> in the low-data regime, we exploit the RoBERTa<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2019</a>)</cite> and BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LARGE</span></sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. 2019</a>)</cite> on CB abd WiC. First, on SST-2 and RTE, fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently outperforms fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, as presented in Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 3.4 Extension Analysis ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. This trend holds when extending RoBERTa<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub> and BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LARGE</span></sub> to CB and WiC, where fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> yields superior performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond learning rates, we also investigate the correlation between training epoch and downstream performance. Taking the CB dataset with only 250 training samples as an example, we observe that for BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>, RoBERTa<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>, and BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LARGE</span></sub>, the performance of fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently improves as training epoch increases. In this case, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves comparable or even superior performance to fine-tuning all biases <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{all}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{all}</annotation></semantics></math>, particularly for BERT<sub id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASE</span></sub>. This suggests that bias-only fine-tuning in low-data regimes requires more training steps. Conversely, with the inappropriate learning rate leading to smaller divergence of bias change, increasing training epochs yields limited improvement for fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these results demonstrate that our findings enable extension to different datasets and <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> without the extra overheads of measuring the importance ranking of bias terms. Furthermore, these extension insights not only show the fine-grained discriminative capability of our bias-efficient approach but also provide valuable guidance on optimizing learning rates and training epochs to enhance downstream performance in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Generalize to Autoregressive <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate the generality of our findings from masked <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> to autoregressive <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, we consider OPT-1.3B and OPT-6.7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2022</a>)</cite> on various benchmarks, including the GLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019b</a>)</cite>, SuperGLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2019a</a>)</cite>, SQuAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajpurkar et al. 2016</a>)</cite>, and DROP datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dua et al. 2019</a>)</cite> covering classification, multiple-choice, and generation tasks. We also compare our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> against various mainstream <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> methods, such as LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, prefix tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang 2021</a>)</cite>, <span title="in-context learning" class="ltx_glossaryref">in-context learning (ICL)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. 2020</a>)</cite> and zero-shot techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. 2020</a>)</cite>, as presented in Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 3.4 Extension Analysis ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. First, regarding bias-selection approaches, fine-tuning <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently performs the best among <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which demonstrates the effective generalization of our bias-efficient approach. Second, our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> requires 30<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and 10<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer parameters than LoRA and prefix tuning, respectively, while maintaining competitive or superior performance across the majority of tasks. Finally, while <span title="in-context learning" class="ltx_glossaryref">ICL</span> and zero-shot techniques do not require fine-tuning, they exhibit inherently inferior performance compared to our <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span>. All these results demonstrate that our proposed <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> approach is able to compete with mainstream <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques aim to fine-tune only a small subset of parameters in <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, thereby significantly reducing resource overheads. Addition-based <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> methods introduce new trainable components into the model architectures. Prefix tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang 2021</a>)</cite> adds trainable prefix tokens to attention blocks; adapter tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Houlsby et al. 2019</a>)</cite> inserts a trainable adapter module into Transformer layers; and prompt-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lester, Al-Rfou, and Constant 2021</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">introduces a sequence of trainable embeddings prepended to the input</span>. Reparameterization-based <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> methods change the representation of the parameter or its update. LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> exploits the low-rank decomposition to approximate the original parameter change, and GaLore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. 2024</a>)</cite> reparameterizes the gradient into low-rank matrices. While effective, these <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are limited by their out-of-the-box usability, with extra requirements for additional configuration and
auxiliary reparameterization.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bias-efficient fine-tuning techniques aim to fine-tune only the bias-terms in <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>. Fine-tuning all biases—as in BitFit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>)</cite>—has been shown to achieve competitive performance with full-parameter fine-tuning, especially in low-data regimes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Logan IV et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. This advantage is attributed in part to the low intrinsic dimensionality of pre-trained <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span>, which allows meaningful adaptation within a restricted subspace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan, Gupta, and Zettlemoyer 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mosbach, Andriushchenko, and Klakow 2021</a>)</cite>. Prior papers reveal that different bias-terms contribute differently during model adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, Kerrigan, and Yang 2024</a>)</cite>. However, the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">link</span> between <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">fine-tuning different bias terms</span> and downstream performance during model adaptation is not clear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng, Kerrigan, and Yang 2024</a>)</cite>. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Moreover, the selective <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> approaches, such as the magnitude of bias change <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ansell et al. 2022</a>)</cite> and empirical Fisher information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sung, Nair, and Raffel 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, provide limited guidance to select the particular bias term.</span> Therefore, the approach for selecting the particular bias term for effectively fine-tuning has been largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bias-only fine-tuning <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">of <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span></span> provides out-of-the-box usability, and demonstrates competitive performance compared to full-parameter fine-tuning empirically, particularly in low-data regimes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaken, Goldberg, and Ravfogel 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Logan IV et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doering et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. In this paper, we propose an approach to select the <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">particular</span> bias term for effective fine-tuning, forming the foundation of <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span>. We extensively evaluate our bias-efficient approach and demonstrate its effectiveness and superiority when compared to the Magnitude and Fisher approaches. Moreover, we extend and generalize our findings and insights to various <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> covering both encoder-only (masked) and decoder-only (autoregressive) <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. 2017</a>)</cite> with sizes ranging from 110M to 6.7B parameters across various downstream tasks, including classification, multiple-choice, and generation tasks. Our experimental evaluation shows that the proposed <span title="bias-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">BEFT</span> approach achieves competitive performance compared to mainstream <span title="parameter-efficient fine-tuning" class="ltx_glossaryref">PEFT</span> techniques.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitation</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we shed light on the importance of fine-tuning bias terms in <span title="large language model" class="ltx_glossaryref">LLMs</span> for unprecedented parameter efficiency. While this paper focuses on importance ranking for the selection of one particular bias term, a deeper investigation into how the importance score relates to downstream performance is required and left for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
